{"name":"Gull Wing","hex":"#abaea9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gull-wing","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative form of gullwing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gull-wing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gull wing","blocks":[{"text":"The gull wing, also known as Polish wing or Puławski wing, is an aircraft wing configuration with a prominent bend in the wing inner section towards the wing root. Its name is derived from the seabirds which it resembles and from the Polish aircraft designer Zygmunt Puławski who started using this design in his planes. Numerous aircraft have incorporated such wings for a diverse range of purposes. The gull wing was commonly used to improve visibility in a high wing arrangement, because such wing could be thinnest by the fuselage, and in theory should limit pilot's view no more than A-pillars of a windscreen in a car body."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/DFS_Habicht_E_1.jpg/330px-DFS_Habicht_E_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gull_wing","wikidata":"Q1521232"},{"title":"Gull","blocks":[{"text":"Gulls, informally called seagulls, are seabirds of the subfamily Larinae. They are most closely related to terns and skimmers, and are placed with them in the family Laridae. They are also related, more distantly, to auks and skuas, and more distantly still to waders. Until the 21st century, most gulls were placed in the genus Larus, but that arrangement is now considered polyphyletic, leading to the readoption and revision of several genera."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Herring_Gull_%28Larus_argentatus%29%2C_Brighton%2C_England.JPG/330px-Herring_Gull_%28Larus_argentatus%29%2C_Brighton%2C_England.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gull","wikidata":"Q2699803"},{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing","wikidata":"Q161358"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Laughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg/500px-Laughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Laughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg/1280px-Laughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Laughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg/500px-Laughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Laughing gull showing the wing shape emulated in gull wing aircraft.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaughing_gull_-_natures_pics.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Beriev_Be-12_Gelenzhik_2Sept2004.jpg/500px-Beriev_Be-12_Gelenzhik_2Sept2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Beriev_Be-12_Gelenzhik_2Sept2004.jpg/500px-Beriev_Be-12_Gelenzhik_2Sept2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beriev Be-12 seaplane with gull wing profile","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeriev_Be-12_Gelenzhik_2Sept2004.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/PZL_P.11c_%2739_-_2%27_%2814338643326%29.jpg/500px-PZL_P.11c_%2739_-_2%27_%2814338643326%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/PZL_P.11c_%2739_-_2%27_%2814338643326%29.jpg/1280px-PZL_P.11c_%2739_-_2%27_%2814338643326%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/PZL_P.11c_%2739_-_2%27_%2814338643326%29.jpg/500px-PZL_P.11c_%2739_-_2%27_%2814338643326%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"PZL P.11c, showing an idea of original Puławski's wing","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APZL_P.11c_'39_-_2'_(14338643326).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Beta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_%28N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001%29_%283-25-2025%29.jpg/500px-Beta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_%28N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001%29_%283-25-2025%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Beta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_%28N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001%29_%283-25-2025%29.jpg/1280px-Beta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_%28N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001%29_%283-25-2025%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Beta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_%28N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001%29_%283-25-2025%29.jpg/500px-Beta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_%28N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001%29_%283-25-2025%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alia CX300 in flight","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeta_ALIA_CX300_in_flight_(N916LF%2C_cn_BT31C-0001)_(3-25-2025).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/StukaRA.jpg/500px-StukaRA.jp